WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.560
- [Dion] At the beginning of last year,

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:04.230
Morgan Stanley equity strategist
Michael Wilson was one

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.630
of the few to predict stock market losses.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:08.940
He was proven right as the S&amp;P 500

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.950
had its worst year since 2008.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.900
- [Mike] But it makes sense
why we're seeing this type

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:13.733
of price action.

00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:16.230
We do have this collision
between tightening Fed

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.400
and slowing growth.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:18.690
This year, he's projecting

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.610
that stocks will fall even further.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:23.250
I'm going to explain just
how far Wilson thinks stocks

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.380
will tumble, why he believes the market

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.790
won't recover this year

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.070
and break down what the numbers tell us

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.500
about the current state of US equities.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:32.670
Let's get into it.

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:33.720
- [Mike] Given that we're about to enter

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:34.800
the last calendar month

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.510
of the first quarter later this week,

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.330
we think the risk of stocks
falling further is high.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.890
- In a note written in late February,

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:44.790
Michael Wilson said he expects
the S&amp;P 500 to fall 25%

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:46.410
from its current levels this year.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.840
That would knock the
benchmark US stock index down

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.930
to levels it hasn't touched
since the spring of 2020.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.310
This is well below the norm

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.500
among equity analysts at the big banks

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.780
and it's got many calling
him the biggest bear

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:58.830
on Wall Street.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:01.740
So why is Wilson so bearish?

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.980
First, he argues that stocks
are currently very expensive,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.990
given the risks that the market is facing.

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:07.920
He points to what's known

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.130
as the equity risk premium
or the expected return

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:14.670
for stocks versus an investment
in US Treasury bonds.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.890
According to Morgan
Stanley's calculations,

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.440
that's at the lowest level since 2007.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.030
Second, Wilson says that even

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:23.190
though stock earnings
are expected to decline,

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:26.190
analysts are being far too
generous with their projections.

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.230
He thinks the companies
are going to underperform

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.870
and that will push stock
prices down even further.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.390
And third, Wall Street's
biggest bear agrees

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:36.060
with many other strategists
who say the Federal Reserve

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.340
is far from done raising US interest rates

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.080
and tightening financial conditions.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.210
If rates go higher than
investors are expecting,

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:45.900
that will put even more pressure
on companies and consumers.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.360
The data certainly suggests
there are reasons to be worried

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.770
about stock prices.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:52.770
Earnings for companies in the
S&amp;P 500 are expected to fall

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.990
by nearly 5% in the latest quarter.

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:57.780
That will be the worst
since third quarter of 2020.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.640
And companies' net profit margins

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.170
or the percentage of every dollar

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.720
of sales they make as
profit is expected to fall

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.430
for the sixth straight quarter.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:07.140
The Fed's latest survey of loan officers

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.420
also shows banks keep
raising their standards

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.030
and fewer companies are
coming to them for loans.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:13.890
There's also the fact that
investors can earn close

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:17.610
to 5% by keeping their money
in one-year US Treasury bonds,

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:18.930
which are considered risk-free

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:21.150
since the US government
has never defaulted.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.700
To be sure, many top money managers,

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:24.840
like the equity strategists

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.480
at JPMorgan believe
stock prices will rebound

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.460
from 2022's big losses

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.170
because the Fed will reverse course

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.390
and start lowering interest
rates later this year.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.040
They believe that will drive money back

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.470
into the market and push
stock prices higher.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.500
But Wilson and other strategists
at places like Barclays,

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.710
Deutsche Bank and Jeffries
see the Fed holding rates high

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:45.463
in order to battle inflation even

00:02:45.463 --> 00:02:47.490
in the face of a painful recession.

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.353
Only time will tell who has it right.

